{\xe \v glfwSwapInterval\:Context reference}
{\xe \v Context reference\:glfwSwapInterval}
\pard\plain \s4\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\cgrid {
{\b 
{\b GLFWAPI} {\b void} glfwSwapInterval (int interval)}}
\par
{\bkmkstart AAAAAABAHC}
{\bkmkend AAAAAABAHC}
{
\pard\plain \s61\li360\sa60\sb30\qj\widctlpar\qj\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
\par
{
{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
Sets the swap interval for the current context. }}\par
{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
This function sets the swap interval for the current OpenGL or OpenGL ES context, i.e. the number of screen updates to wait from the time {\b glfwSwapBuffers} was called before swapping the buffers and returning. This is sometimes called {\i vertical synchronization} , {\i vertical retrace synchronization}  or just {\i vsync} .\par
A context that supports either of the {\f2 WGL_EXT_swap_control_tear}  and {\f2 GLX_EXT_swap_control_tear}  extensions also accepts {\i negative}  swap intervals, which allows the driver to swap immediately even if a frame arrives a little bit late. You can check for these extensions with {\b glfwExtensionSupported}.\par
A context must be current on the calling thread. Calling this function without a current context will cause a {\b GLFW_NO_CURRENT_CONTEXT} error.\par
This function does not apply to Vulkan. If you are rendering with Vulkan, see the present mode of your swapchain instead.\par
{{\s5\sb90\sa30\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\fs16\cgrid 
Parameters\par}
\pard\plain \s81\li360\widctlpar\ql\adjustright \fs20\cgrid \trowd \trgaph108\trleft426\tblind426\trbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \trbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \trbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \trbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \trbrdrh\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \trbrdrv\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 
\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \clbrdrr \brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \cltxlrtb \cellx1224
\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \clbrdrr \brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \cltxlrtb \cellx3061
\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \clbrdrr \brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf15 \cltxlrtb \cellx8748
\pard \widctlpar\intbl\adjustright
{in\cell }{{\i interval} \cell }{The minimum number of screen updates to wait for until the buffers are swapped by {\b glfwSwapBuffers}.\cell }
{\row }
}
@errors Possible errors include {\b GLFW_NOT_INITIALIZED}, {\b GLFW_NO_CURRENT_CONTEXT} and {\b GLFW_PLATFORM_ERROR}.\par
{{\s5\sb90\sa30\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\fs16\cgrid 
Remarks\par}\pard\plain \s82\li720\widctlpar\ql\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\s17 \sa60 \sb30
This function is not called during context creation, leaving the swap interval set to whatever is the default for that API. This is done because some swap interval extensions used by GLFW do not allow the swap interval to be reset to zero once it has been set to a non-zero value.\par
Some GPU drivers do not honor the requested swap interval, either because of a user setting that overrides the application's request or due to bugs in the driver.\par
}}@thread_safety This function may be called from any thread.\par
{{\s5\sb90\sa30\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\fs16\cgrid 
